t\n\nWe study the Randić index for cactus graphs. It is conjectured to be bounded below by radius (for other than an even path), and it is known to obey several bounds based on diameter. We study radius and diameter for cacti then verify the radius bound and strengthen two diameter bounds for cacti. Along the way, we produce several other bounds for the Randić index in terms of graph size, order, and valency for several special classes of graphs, including chemical nontrivial cacti and cacti with starlike BC-trees.\n\n... Read more\n\n## Key takeaways\n\nImage: sparkles\n\nAI\n\n  1. Strengthened bounds for the Randić index R in terms of cactus graph radius and diameter are established.\n  2. The conjecture states Randić index R is bounded below by the radius for non-even paths.\nRANDIĆ INDEX, RADIUS, AND DIAMETER FOR CACTUS GRAPHS arXiv:2107.00071v2 [math.CO] 14 Jul 2021 MARGARET I. DOIG Abstract. We study the Randić index for cactus graphs. It is conjectured to be bounded below by radius (for other than an even path), and it is known to obey several bounds based on diameter. We study radius and diameter for cacti then verify the radius bound and strengthen two diameter bounds for cacti. Along the way, we produce several other bounds for the Randić index in terms of graph size, order, and valency for several special classes of graphs, including chemical nontrivial cacti and cacti with starlike BC-trees. 1. Introduction Scientific motivation. A number of molecular properties appear to depend on the shape of a molecule and vary even between different arrangements of the same atoms; for example, the boiling points of hexane isomers appear to be correlated to the surface area of the molecular cloud, which reflects the degree and location of branching of the molecule. In 1975, Milan Randić proposed a molecular branch- ing index based entirely on molecular graphs in an attempt to mathematically characterize branching in a way consistent with boiling point and other structure- related properties such as the enthalpy of formation of alkanes and the relationship of vapor pressure to temperature. The Randić index of a graph G is X R = R(G) = w(e) e Date: July 16, 2021. 2010 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary 05C09, Secondary 92E10. Key words and phrases. connectivity index; Randić index; cactus; radius; diameter. Supported by CURAS Summer Faculty Research Fund. 1 where the sum runs over all edges e and w(e) is a weight assigned to each edge, that is, if e = uv is an edge in E and du is the degree of a vertex u, then 1 w(e) = √ . du dv This invariant was recast by Caporossi, Gurman, Hansen, and Pavlovic in 2003 [CGHP03] as n − n0 X ∗ R= − w (e) 2 e∈E where n is the number of vertices and n0 the number of isolated vertices, and w∗ (e) is a measure of the asymmetry of edge weights, that is, \u0012 \u00132 1 1 1 (1) w∗ (e) = √ −√ . 2 du dv An immediate result, as indicated by the authors, is an upper bound on the Randić index: n R≤ 2 and, in fact, this bound is attained by regular graphs Kn , where every vertex in each component has the same degree and therefore every edge has w∗ = 0. Due to its success modeling physicochemical properties of molecules, there is a great deal of interest in approximating the Randić index and understanding how it changes under certain structural alterations, or bounding it on particular classes of graphs. It has been compared to the minimum/maximum degrees of a graph [BE98, SS18, DP13, LPD+ 13], chromatic index [LS10a], average path length [CH00], graph eigenvalues [ADLP98, AHZ06, EW15], graph matching [AHZ06], and radius and diameter. Our efforts focus on the last two, which we will define below. Radius was first explicitly connected to the Randić index in the ground-breaking work in the late 1980s by Fajtlowicz, who used a computer search of a large database of graphs and invariants to conjecture possible relationships between invariants. He proposed the radius as a possible lower bound for R: Conjecture 1.1. [Faj88] Let G be a graph. If it is an even path, then: √ 3 R−r ≥ 2− 2 and, otherwise, R − r ≥ 0. Note the original conjecture was that R ≥ r − 1, which was later modified to R ≥ r for graphs other than even paths, where R√≈ r − 0.1. Caporossi and Hansen verified R − r ≥ 2 − 32 for trees in 2000 [CH00]; Cygan, Pilipczuk, and Škrekovski R − r ≥ − 21 for chemical graphs in 2012 [CPŠ12]; Liu and Gutman R − r ≥ −1 for several special classes of graphs, including unicyclic and bicyclic graphs, in 2009 [LG09]; You and Liu R − r ≥ −1 for tricyclic graphs and R − r ≥ 0 for biregular graphs and graphs up to 10 vertices in 2009 [YL09]. We complete the proof of the sharper bound R − r ≥ 0 in Theorems 5.3 and 5.5 for cacti (graphs in which no two cycles share an edge): 2 Corollary 1.2. Let G be a cactus. If G is not an even path, R − r ≥ 0. In fact, if G has k > 0 cycles, \u0010√ \u0011 R − r ≥ (k − 1) 2−1 and, if G has m > 0 bridges, r ! \u0010√ \u0011 1 2 1 R − r ≥ (k − 1) 2−1 +m √ + −1 − . Zhifu You and Bolian Liu. On a conjecture of the Randić index. Discret. Appl. Math., 157(8):1766-1772, 2009.\n  27. Yiting Yang and Linyuan Lu. The Randić index and the diameter of graphs. Discrete Math., 311(14):1333-1343, 2011.\n  28. Meng Zhang and Bolian Liu. On a conjecture about the Randić index and diam- eter. MATCH Commun. Math. Comput. Chem., 64(2):433-442, 2010. Department of Mathematics, Creighton University Email address: [email protected]\n\nAbout the author\n\nImage\n\nMargaret Doig\n\nCreighton University, Faculty Member\n\nadd Follow mail Message\n\nPapers\n\n12\n\nView all papers from Margaret Doigarrow_forward\n\nA connected graph in which no edge lies on more than one cycle is called a cactus graph (also known as Husimi tree). A bond incident degree (BID) index of a graph G is defined as ∑ u v ∈ E G f d G u , d G v , where d G w denotes the degree of a vertex w of G , E G is the edge set of G , and f is a real-valued symmetric function. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Doig_M in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ADoig_M+in%3Amath)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; We also identify a subgraph which respects the block structure of the original graph and realizes the same vertex radius, and we use it to verify that cactus graphs satisfy a conjectured bound between vertex radius and the Randic index, an invariant from mathematical chemistry. ... ~Doig}, title = {{R}andic index, radius, and diameter for cactus graphs}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2107.00071}, note = {arXiv:2107.00071v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n  * Block eccentricity, a radius bound, and an application to the Randić index\n\nMargaret I. Doig\n\nSep 22 2023 math.CO arXiv:2309.11613v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2309.11613, author = {Margaret I.~Doig}, title = {{B}lock eccentricity, a radius bound, and an application to the {R}andić index}, year = {2023}, eprint = {2309.11613}, note = {arXiv:2309.11613v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe propose a framework for thinking about eccentricity in terms of blocks. We extend the familiar definitions of radius and center to blocks and verify that a central block contains all central points. We classify graphs into two types depending upon the relationship between block radius and vertex radius and between central blocks and central vertices; from this we derive a new lower bound on diameter in terms of the diameter of the central block. We also identify a subgraph which respects the block structure of the original graph and realizes the same vertex radius, and we use it to verify that cactus graphs satisfy a conjectured bound between vertex radius and the Randic index, an invariant from mathematical chemistry.\n\n  * Randic index, radius, and diameter for cactus graphs\n\nMargaret I. Doig\n\nJul 02 2021 math.CO arXiv:2107.00071v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2107.00